course: dictionary
lesson: 5098
title: Interleave: putting things between each other
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To interleave things is to put them between each other, one by one, like mixing two piles of cards. «Two books with their pages interleaved» means the pages of one book were slid in between the pages of the other, one at a time.
explain: The clues help you. The words «slid in between each other, one by one» tell you what interleave means. The second sentence, «two interleaved phone books», uses the same idea: the books were pushed into each other, page by page.
explain: In study skills, interleaving means mixing different kinds of practice problems together, instead of doing one kind all at once. You may see the phrases «interleave A with B» and «interleaved practice». Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Interleave is a verb: interleave, interleaves, interleaved, interleaving.
fact: «Interleave» means to put things between each other, in turn.
fact: To interleave a book is to put blank or extra pages between its pages.
fact: «Interleaved practice» means mixing different kinds of problems together, and research suggests this helps learning.
fact: In computing, interleaving means mixing two streams of data.
exercise: «Two books with their pages interleaved» means the pages were | Glued shut so they cannot open | Cut into small squares | *Slid in between each other, one by one | Printed in colour
exercise: Ms Okafor says, «Let's interleave maths and spelling practice.» What does she mean? | Throw the practice away | Do all the maths first, then all the spelling | Stop both and have a break | *Take turns between maths and spelling
exercise: Which word means to put things between each other, in turn? | *interleave | interrupt | interfere | inherit
test: «The two piles of cards were interleaved, one by one.» What does interleaved mean here? | *Put between each other in turn | Thrown away | Counted twice | Sorted by colour
test: Tom interleaves his reading and maths homework. What is he doing? | Doing maths in the dark | *Mixing two kinds of practice together | Doing all the reading first | Skipping his homework
exam: Ruby says interleaved practice helps her learn. Which best explains why? | It means doing one kind of problem all day | *It mixes different problems, which research suggests helps learning | It makes the problems longer | Ruby says it is more fun
